00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.770
But first of all, why did
Google actually go around

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.220
and actually introduce Istio
as an open source project?

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:00.540
So let's talk a little
bit about the need

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.940
for an actual Service Mesh.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:09.220
So everyone knows
microservices are awesome.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:14.260
If you think about traditional
applications, monoliths,

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:17.050
you actually have all
your business logic

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:19.800
in one chunk of code.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:24.310
You pick one language,
you write your code.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:27.040
To a certain extent, if
your application is small

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.030
and your team's small
it's actually easier.

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:31.990
You have one thing to deploy,
you have one piece of code

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:34.210
that's doing many things,
but at the end of the day,

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:35.668
again, it's just
one piece of code.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:43.210
Where monoliths become
more problematic

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:45.310
is if you need to scale
certain capabilities

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.130
within that codebase
independently.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.289
It becomes challenging.

00:01:50.289 --> 00:01:51.955
Everyone's working
on the same codebase.

00:01:51.955 --> 00:01:53.690
So if you want to
change one thing,

00:01:53.690 --> 00:02:00.620
you're still pushing the whole
application, so microservices.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.720
So the benefits around
microservices, your developers

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.910
can actually focus on a
specific set of business logic.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:12.940
You can pick a
language that is more

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.760
applicable for that
particular business logic.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.270
So for example, you might use
Ruby to write the front end,

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:25.090
you might use C++ to
write the back end, or Go,

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:26.900
so it gives you a
lot of flexibility.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.140
It also means you can
scale those microservices

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:30.610
independently of each other.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:35.080
You can also deploy them
independently of each other.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.767
And as the developer
of the front end,

00:02:36.767 --> 00:02:38.350
I actually don't
need to know anything

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:41.800
about the ETL pipeline.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.560
All I really need to
understand is what interfaces

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.660
or what APIs I
need to connect to

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.370
and what I need to do
to make that happen.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.860
So it actually
becomes very-- there's

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:55.280
some definite advantages here.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:01.070
But what happens when you start
to really scale microservices?

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:05.240
You deploy multiple
regions, you actually

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:08.000
have a very large
number of microservices.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.590
You go from maybe five, 10, 100,
maybe to 1,000 microservices.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:15.020
At that point it becomes
very difficult to actually

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:18.110
understand exactly which
microservice is talking

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:19.700
to which other microservice.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:24.810
It's hard to understand
if a specific--

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.560
you've only got to have
one microservice fail,

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.710
and your application,
effectively, is down.

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:34.130
So it's sometimes hard
to determine exactly

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:35.980
which microservice is
causing the problem

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:39.235
or what's going on
within that environment.

00:03:39.235 --> 00:03:40.610
Another thing
that's interesting,

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:43.940
too, is if you're writing
these microservices

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:47.930
in different languages,
each implementation

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:51.179
of a given protocol might
be slightly different.

00:03:51.179 --> 00:03:52.970
You've got different
developers writing it.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:57.680
As a result of that, actually
having the same implementation

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.630
across the environment
can sometimes

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:03.210
be difficult and
hard to maintain.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:06.170
So maybe microservices
aren't so cool after all.

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:08.300
There's some challenges
there, right?

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:10.190
As that application
scales, you've

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:12.140
really got to start
working on how

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:15.480
to control that environment.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.334
So what do we do in
this type of industry

00:04:18.334 --> 00:04:19.709
when there's a
problem like this?

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:21.350
We developed tools, effectively.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:24.650
So welcome to Service Mesh.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:26.890
What is Istio?

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:30.380
So Istio is an open source
project, originally kicked off

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.680
by Google and IBM.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.810
It's actually developing
a huge amount of traction.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.024
There's a lot of
interest in Istio.

00:04:39.024 --> 00:04:41.440
Has anyone, actually in this
room-- just a show of hands--

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.350
obviously, based on
the amount of times

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:46.435
Istio has been mentioned
at Google Next,

00:04:46.435 --> 00:04:48.060
I'm pretty sure
everyone's heard of it.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:49.970
Who's actually used it?

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:52.010
OK, excellent,
there's quite a few.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:54.570
Fantastic.

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:57.460
And Istio is actually a
fairly broad framework.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:59.510
It provides a lot
of capabilities.

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:03.170
So let's talk a little bit
about what that actually--

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:06.810
what we can actually
do with Istio.

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:09.290
So it's effectively an
open service platform

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:11.960
that allows you to manage
the service interaction.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:14.750
So you basically start
thinking about services

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.715
first within your environment.

00:05:17.715 --> 00:05:19.340
The other key thing
here is it actually

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:22.250
works across both containerized
environments as well

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:23.780
as VM-based workloads.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:29.270
And it could potentially
be on prem as well as

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:31.688
on cloud or multi-cloud.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.840
There's three main tenants
that Istio provides

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.340
from a value perspective.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:46.700
The first tenant is
related to providing

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:48.760
uniform observability.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:51.170
With Istio you can
effectively get a common set

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:53.210
of metrics that are
automatically implemented

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:55.850
across your environment without
actually having to do that

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:57.560
from a developer perspective.

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.629
The developer now
can just focus on--

00:05:59.629 --> 00:06:01.670
you effectively get this
from the infrastructure.

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:04.330
The developer can focus
on the business logic.

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:06.770
You get operational
agility, the ability

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:12.630
to change based on a level seven
context how you want traffic

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:15.590
to traverse through that
mesh and what you actually

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:16.890
want to do with that.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:21.470
You can also start providing a
policy-driven security posture.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:25.430
Provide actually your
service-to-service security

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:27.530
as well as--

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:31.490
and this effectively
is infrastructure,

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:33.704
that the infrastructure
can provide you.

00:06:33.704 --> 00:06:35.120
Your software
developers now don't

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.360
have to worry about implementing
these type of capabilities.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.890
Let's take a little
bit of a closer look.

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:45.050
Istio architectural
components-- this

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:49.520
is, I think pretty much every
Istio presentation I've seen

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:52.550
tends to have this diagram
or something similar to it.

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:54.830
There are three major
control plane components

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:56.420
that's a part of Istio.

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:58.400
Pilot, which we'll
talk about, which

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:02.000
effectively controls your
routing and your forwarding

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:03.350
policy.

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:05.000
Mixer, which is
actually something

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:06.083
that's pretty interesting.

00:07:06.083 --> 00:07:09.390
It provides an extensible
model, and we'll talk about some

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:10.940
of the things you
can do with Mixer.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:12.966
And then you've
got Citadel, which

00:07:12.966 --> 00:07:20.269
provides a lot of your
security aspects of Istio.

00:07:20.269 --> 00:07:21.560
Let's quickly get through this.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:28.439
What can you actually do with
security, Istio security?

00:07:28.439 --> 00:07:30.480
There was actually a really
good talk yesterday--

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:31.950
I don't know if any
of you got to catch it

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:33.930
but it was a talk about
security in depth.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:36.570
You know, security is not
just a perimeter problem.

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:38.580
One of the drawbacks or
one of the things you

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:40.830
need to be aware of when
you go to microservices

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:42.830
is now you've actually
opened all these channels

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:45.800
of communication between
services and between parts

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:46.910
of your application.

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:48.493
You need to be able
to lock them down.

00:07:48.493 --> 00:07:50.466
So you can use mTLS for that.

00:07:50.466 --> 00:07:51.840
We also have the
ability-- and we

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:57.180
do that effectively based on
service identity and channel

00:07:57.180 --> 00:07:58.150
attributes.

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:01.840
So it's more than actually
just providing mTLS.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:05.210
We can also control
the level of access.

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:09.790
So service A can talk to
service B at full access.

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:13.225
Service C can talk to service
B at maybe read-only access.

00:08:13.225 --> 00:08:14.850
So you can do things
like that as well.

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:23.740
If you think about
why you support mTLS,

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:25.530
this actually gives
the ability via Istio

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:29.310
to basically do a policy-driven
encryption in transit.

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:30.630
But that's not all.

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:33.320
The other key thing is
around strong authentication,

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:39.900
actually authenticating based
on a strong service identity.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:40.750
This is important.

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:44.790
So I can actually
make sure that if I

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:49.230
have a compromised service, that
I can't replay that identity

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:50.805
to a different service.

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.480
Let's talk a little
bit about Mixer.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:59.540
Mixer actually provides
really good extensibility.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:03.650
I can effectively use Mixer to
interconnect to any back end

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:05.360
I want to.

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:07.900
I can use Mixer as a
way to actually pull

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:11.260
in policy into my mesh,
and we'll talk about that.

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:13.430
We'll talk about that
specifically in the context

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:17.000
of API management.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:19.040
Pilot-- Pilot gives
us the ability

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:22.830
to basically push not only
the routing rules but also

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:28.310
security policy to the proxies,
in this example, Envoy proxies.

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:29.900
This gives us the
ability to do things

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:32.510
like I can do A/B testing.

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:34.790
I can do canary
rollouts, again, all

00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:37.420
based on level seven context.

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:39.530
So this is kind of what
Istio, some of the things

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:42.050
that Istio provides.

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:44.573
One of the key
things here is we've

00:09:44.573 --> 00:09:46.640
got a little bit of an app.

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:49.080
So we have an app.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.695
This is actually an app that
was also shown at the keynote.

00:09:51.695 --> 00:09:53.570
This is the Hipster app,
and this is actually

00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:54.361
what it looks like.

00:09:54.361 --> 00:09:57.530
So there's around
11, 12 microservices.

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:00.010
Some are written in Go,
some are written in Node.js,

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:02.570
some are written in Python.

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:05.460
But this is just like a typical
little microservices app.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:08.043
Let's have a quick look at this,
and let's have a look at what

00:10:08.043 --> 00:10:09.680
we can do with Istio with this.

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:12.300
So let's transition to the demo.

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:16.700
So here we have
the Hipster Store.

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:20.060
This is everything-- I think
they said on the keynote--

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:22.954
everything the hipster
requires for a good lifestyle.

00:10:22.954 --> 00:10:24.620
So I'm going to
actually-- first of all,

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:27.220
I'm going to grab a typewriter,
because I'm actually

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:29.090
going to go and do some typing.

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:31.160
I'm going to need
some coffee, so I'll

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:33.720
get a mug for my coffee.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:38.510
And then maybe a little
bit of music as well.

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:42.000
I'll add that all to my cart.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:45.540
Oh, that's-- Wi-Fi
demons have got me again.

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:52.750
OK, so here we go.

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:54.580
Let's see if I can view my cart.

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:56.560
Oh, we're good,
back up and running.

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:00.310
Nothing like Wi-Fi in a
conference environment

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:03.960
to do a demo, just when you
can't possibly be more nervous.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:05.884
Anyway, so here we go.

00:11:05.884 --> 00:11:07.550
So we've actually got
our Hipster Store.

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:12.336
We've placed our order and
we're back up and running.

00:11:12.336 --> 00:11:14.960
So let's actually have a look at
what this actually looks like.

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:18.970
So I have a single cluster
running in Kubernetes.

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:25.180
It's just a relatively
small cluster.

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:26.210
On that cluster.

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:28.485
I have a bunch of workloads.

00:11:28.485 --> 00:11:30.610
You'll actually see some
of the same workloads that

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:32.920
were actually in
that diagram before.

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:39.572
We have like the front
end, the email service,

00:11:39.572 --> 00:11:41.530
but you'll also see a
bunch of Istio components

00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:43.190
that are here as well.

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:46.240
The other thing that we can do
is if we drill into one here,

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:49.570
you'll also notice that, OK, so
for this particular service--

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:51.820
I'm actually going to drift
into the pod for a second.

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:52.791
You'll also notice--

00:11:52.791 --> 00:11:54.290
I'm doing this a
little bit quickly,

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:57.160
but for this particular
service, we also

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:02.020
have in the same
container, or same pod,

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:04.780
we actually have a sidecar.

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:07.090
So this is effectively
a transparent sidecar

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:10.830
that's running co-located
with that service.

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:14.560
OK, so what can we actually do?

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:19.422
We've got our service running,
our sidecars are all deployed.

00:12:19.422 --> 00:12:21.130
Let's actually have
a look at what we can

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:22.547
do from a metrics perspective.

00:12:22.547 --> 00:12:24.130
So I'm actually going
to come in here.

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:26.500
I'm going to actually--

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:33.770
I now have a bunch of
standard Istio metrics

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:35.572
that now are just
available to me.

00:12:35.572 --> 00:12:37.280
I'm actually using
Stackdriver, but these

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:39.620
could be piped into any
back end that you want.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:42.050
A lot of people use
Prometheus, Grafana

00:12:42.050 --> 00:12:44.630
as their standard
back end of choice.

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:47.960
So you can pick your
back end of choice.

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:50.690
I can actually look
at those metrics

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:53.130
very cleanly and very simply.

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:54.630
The other thing
that I can do is now

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:56.910
I can actually get
relational information.

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:58.620
So this is the exact
same application.

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:01.790
Now I'm looking at a
topology view of what

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:03.584
my services are connected to.

00:13:03.584 --> 00:13:05.750
So if I look at front end
and I highlight front end,

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:07.725
I can now see my
exact dependency.

00:13:07.725 --> 00:13:09.600
The other thing that's
really key about this,

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:11.141
so if you think
about it, now if I've

00:13:11.141 --> 00:13:13.120
got a really large
application, maybe you

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:19.310
know hundreds of microservices,
and I'm not exactly sure what

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:22.310
my specific service is talking
to, I can actually use a tool

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:24.590
like this now to
