00:18:45.070 --> 00:18:46.444
At this point,
I'm actually going

00:18:46.444 --> 00:18:47.957
to introduce Nandan to come up.

00:18:47.957 --> 00:18:50.040
He's going to actually
talk a little bit about API

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:51.750
management.

00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:54.910
So we can actually cross
back to the slides.

00:18:54.910 --> 00:18:56.390
NANDAN SRIDHAR:
Thank you, David.

00:18:56.390 --> 00:18:59.910
All right, so I'm going
to talk a bit about how

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:02.536
API management fits with Istio.

00:19:02.536 --> 00:19:05.080
One of the questions
I get asked a lot is.

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:08.640
If I'm doing microservices,
do I need API management?

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:11.730
Or if I've got Istio
managing my services do

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:14.340
I still need API management?

00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:18.670
Well, let's start with
what is API management?

00:19:18.670 --> 00:19:20.350
Let's take a simple example.

00:19:20.350 --> 00:19:22.215
I've got service
A, and service A

00:19:22.215 --> 00:19:26.250
is going to call service B.
If service A and B are really

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:29.340
built by the same team,
then no, not really, you

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:30.480
don't need API management.

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:33.080
You want some
documentation for service B

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:36.030
so it's well documented so
people who wrote service A

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:37.430
know how to invoke it.

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:40.440
But most use cases
are not that simple.

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:42.210
In a very large
enterprise, you're

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:45.390
going to have many, many
teams building services.

00:19:45.390 --> 00:19:49.260
Service A and B are not
built by the same team.

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:50.850
The team that built
service B wants

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:53.183
to share that service with
the rest of the organization,

00:19:53.183 --> 00:19:56.800
with third parties, with
partners, who knows what else.

00:19:56.800 --> 00:20:01.050
So enterprises want to have
a service catalog where APIs

00:20:01.050 --> 00:20:03.190
are published to that catalog.

00:20:03.190 --> 00:20:05.280
And this is different
from a runtime catalog

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:06.540
that Istio provides.

00:20:06.540 --> 00:20:10.570
A catalog that Istio provides
is for Envoy and the sidecars

00:20:10.570 --> 00:20:11.760
to find services.

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:13.680
This is more of a
catalog for humans,

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:17.920
for developers to come and
read about that service.

00:20:17.920 --> 00:20:20.460
So in addition to
documentation and a catalog,

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:22.680
the next thing that
enterprises would want

00:20:22.680 --> 00:20:25.650
is some way of
governing who has access

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:27.400
to which types of services.

00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:30.360
So once you onboard a developer,
so some sort of developer

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:32.460
identity is
established, you then

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:36.150
want to control which developer
or developers can get access

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:38.100
to which services.

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:41.040
And as an API provider,
I want to know which

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:43.890
applications consume my API.

00:20:43.890 --> 00:20:46.770
Is it the branch application
or is it the call center

00:20:46.770 --> 00:20:48.990
application or is
the mobile app?

00:20:48.990 --> 00:20:52.950
Which application is consuming
the services that I built?

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:55.470
Who are the developers who
are writing applications

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:57.330
that are consuming my services?

00:20:57.330 --> 00:21:01.830
That's the kind of view I want
to have as an API provider.

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:04.380
Some of those APIs
you may even want

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:07.050
to productize and monetize.

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:10.050
APIs are quickly
becoming another channel

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:13.570
through which enterprises are
able to monetize their data.

00:21:13.570 --> 00:21:17.250
So when you want to productize
and monetize those APIs, then

00:21:17.250 --> 00:21:19.510
again, API management
is important.

00:21:19.510 --> 00:21:22.050
So these five factors
help you decide

00:21:22.050 --> 00:21:24.780
if a service needs to
grow beyond your team,

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:26.610
needs to be shared
with others, needs

00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:31.410
to eventually be monetized, then
API management is important.

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:33.250
There's another aspect.

00:21:33.250 --> 00:21:34.890
There are certain
capabilities that you

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:38.290
don't want every single
service provider to build.

00:21:38.290 --> 00:21:40.670
There are certain
cross-cutting concerns.

00:21:40.670 --> 00:21:43.440
You've seen this in the
form of API gateways

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:46.980
that typically do the job
of rate limiting or quotas.

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:48.780
They do the job of caching.

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:51.210
They do the job of some
transformation mediation.

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:53.220
There are some activities
that you don't want

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:54.730
every single team to build.

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:56.280
So you build some
sort of a service

00:21:56.280 --> 00:21:57.900
that they can all leverage.

00:21:57.900 --> 00:22:01.410
All these are factors that
go into API management.

00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:03.990
So how do they compare?

00:22:03.990 --> 00:22:05.880
From our point of
view, you need Istio

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:07.600
to manage all your services.

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:09.870
That could be a database
service, a caching

00:22:09.870 --> 00:22:13.530
service, your web pages,
these are all services.

00:22:13.530 --> 00:22:15.390
Some of those
services become APIs

00:22:15.390 --> 00:22:20.590
when they're shared across your
teams across the organizations.

00:22:20.590 --> 00:22:22.890
And at that point you
need API management

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:25.050
to manage those APIs.

00:22:25.050 --> 00:22:28.290
So all APIs are services.

00:22:28.290 --> 00:22:30.420
Some of those
services become APIs

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:32.630
and you'll need to manage them.

00:22:32.630 --> 00:22:37.220
So how, then, do we compare the
capabilities of API management

00:22:37.220 --> 00:22:38.840
and service management?

00:22:38.840 --> 00:22:41.090
I'm going to go through
a couple of examples.

00:22:41.090 --> 00:22:43.970
On the face of it, they have
very similar capabilities,

00:22:43.970 --> 00:22:45.470
but when you peel
the layers, you'll

00:22:45.470 --> 00:22:47.690
see that there are some
interesting differences.

00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:50.790
If you take monitoring
as an example,

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:54.470
service management focus is from
a production operator's point

00:22:54.470 --> 00:22:55.100
of view.

00:22:55.100 --> 00:22:57.110
They're more interested
in latencies.

00:22:57.110 --> 00:22:58.950
They're interested
in error codes.

00:22:58.950 --> 00:23:00.950
That's what their interests
are, and to pinpoint

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:03.250
where exactly which
service is failing

00:23:03.250 --> 00:23:06.980
or is behaving in a bad way.

00:23:06.980 --> 00:23:09.260
But from an API
provider's point of view,

00:23:09.260 --> 00:23:12.830
they want to know who are the
top five applications consuming

00:23:12.830 --> 00:23:13.617
my service?

00:23:13.617 --> 00:23:15.200
That's sort of a
business-centric view

00:23:15.200 --> 00:23:17.480
of who are my top consumers?

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:21.050
What will happen if I make
a change to a certain API?

00:23:21.050 --> 00:23:22.790
Which applications are impacted?

00:23:22.790 --> 00:23:26.480
That an API provider
would be interested in?

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:29.570
Even if you take an
example like rate limiting,

00:23:29.570 --> 00:23:32.360
Istio will provide rate
limiting at a service level,

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:34.040
say 1,000 TPS.

00:23:34.040 --> 00:23:36.980
That's how much my
service can handle.

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:40.070
But what someone in product
management can decide

00:23:40.070 --> 00:23:42.740
is, I want my call
center applications

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:46.820
to get higher priority than,
say, my mobile application.

00:23:46.820 --> 00:23:48.640
Because I have
more revenue impact

00:23:48.640 --> 00:23:50.900
if my branch services
are not working

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:52.500
as opposed to my mobile app.

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:56.240
That sort of a decision of
prioritizing your requests

00:23:56.240 --> 00:23:59.000
can be done through
API management.

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:02.000
And of course there are some
capabilities like monetization

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:05.000
that would only be applicable
within the API management

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:06.110
realm.

00:24:06.110 --> 00:24:10.240
So how then do we make
API management work

00:24:10.240 --> 00:24:12.110
in an Istio service mesh?

00:24:12.110 --> 00:24:14.600
Let's bring you back full
circle to what the Istio

00:24:14.600 --> 00:24:16.460
architecture looks like.

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:18.100
This is the Istio service mesh.

00:24:18.100 --> 00:24:20.870
Its got all the standard
components and Citadel

00:24:20.870 --> 00:24:23.585
or security is not
here, but easily it

00:24:23.585 --> 00:24:24.980
could have been in this picture.

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:27.680
You're seeing Mixer,
you're seeing Pilot,

00:24:27.680 --> 00:24:30.350
and you're seeing the
sidecar and ingress.

00:24:30.350 --> 00:24:33.020
The perfect point to
introduce API management

00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:35.840
here is at Mixer.

00:24:35.840 --> 00:24:40.260
Mixer is a policy advisor
to the Envoy sidecars.

00:24:40.260 --> 00:24:44.720
It tells Envoy when to
allow or not allow traffic.

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:48.920
That's extremely key when you're
enforcing rules or policies.

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:52.970
Also, Mixer is the point that
is collecting telemetry data

00:24:52.970 --> 00:24:56.270
that you can ship it off
to your API dashboards.

00:24:56.270 --> 00:24:59.570
What you'll see here
is an implementation

00:24:59.570 --> 00:25:04.400
of that API management in a
service mesh through Apigee.

00:25:04.400 --> 00:25:07.130
Apigee has extended
the Mixer architecture

00:25:07.130 --> 00:25:10.460
to be aware of Apigee
API management rules.

00:25:10.460 --> 00:25:13.210
So as a developer you can
define those rules centrally

00:25:13.210 --> 00:25:16.700
in the Apigee control plane
of productizing your APIs,

00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:19.010
defining quotas,
finding entitlements

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:22.280
of who can access your
APIs, also publish

00:25:22.280 --> 00:25:24.470
those services to
the developer portal

00:25:24.470 --> 00:25:28.220
to determine who can see those
APIs, the documentation for it,

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:29.330
and so on.

00:25:29.330 --> 00:25:32.000
And when those services
are now in the portal,

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:33.920
consumers can come
and subscribe to it

00:25:33.920 --> 00:25:37.809
and get developer keys
to access that service.

00:25:37.809 --> 00:25:40.100
Once you have defined these
rules in the Apigee control

00:25:40.100 --> 00:25:43.220
plane, Mixer downloads
that information

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:46.190
and now those
Apigee rules are now

00:25:46.190 --> 00:25:47.890
present in the service mesh.

00:25:47.890 --> 00:25:50.770
The enforcement now happens
at the sidecar level

00:25:50.770 --> 00:25:52.700
or at the ingress
level, depending

00:25:52.700 --> 00:25:54.920
on how you have defined it.

00:25:54.920 --> 00:25:59.330
So let's take a look at
this with an example.

00:25:59.330 --> 00:26:01.790
We had the Hipster app.

00:26:01.790 --> 00:26:03.290
Let's say the
requirements changed.

00:26:03.290 --> 00:26:05.060
It was mostly a
web-based application

00:26:05.060 --> 00:26:08.480
that had a whole bunch of
microservices backing it,

00:26:08.480 --> 00:26:11.360
and now we have a
new requirement.

00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:15.560
The shipping service there also
allows me to track shipments,

00:26:15.560 --> 00:26:18.770
and I want to expose this
tracking API, if you will,

00:26:18.770 --> 00:26:21.200
part of the shipping
service externally.

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:23.730
So it can be invoked
not just through the UI

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:26.990
but through mobile app,
through something else.

00:26:26.990 --> 00:26:29.790
How would we go ahead
and protect this service?

00:26:29.790 --> 00:26:31.665
So that's what we'll
do with the demo next.

00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:36.190
Switch to the computer, please.

00:26:36.190 --> 00:26:38.410
All right, there it is.

00:26:38.410 --> 00:26:43.900
So let's go first and
get the ingress IP

00:26:43.900 --> 00:26:47.880
so I can go and access this API.

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:49.240
Let's go to that.

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:52.210
That's going to
store the ingress IP.

00:26:52.210 --> 00:26:56.200
Let's make sure I have
the IP address here.

00:26:56.200 --> 00:26:58.450
I have my IP address
for the ingress.

00:26:58.450 --> 00:27:00.820
Now let's go ahead
and access this API.

00:27:04.054 --> 00:27:05.000
All right.

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:08.250
So I got access to my API.

00:27:08.250 --> 00:27:11.270
Now that's not a good thing,
because I accessed that API

00:27:11.270 --> 00:27:13.760
by providing absolutely
no credentials.

00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:15.930
So I want to go and fix that.

00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:21.230
So let me go ahead and
apply a rule to change that.

00:27:21.230 --> 00:27:25.450
I'll explain in a second
what those rules are.

00:27:25.450 --> 00:27:27.320
There are actually three
files here that I'm

00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:28.490
going to walk you through.

00:27:28.490 --> 00:27:30.170
Two of them you do one time.

00:27:30.170 --> 00:27:33.710
This is a one-time setup to
make your mesh API management

00:27:33.710 --> 00:27:35.730
affair, if you will.

00:27:35.730 --> 00:27:38.390
So the first is a
definitions file.

00:27:38.390 --> 00:27:42.500
The definitions file creates
custom resource definitions

00:27:42.500 --> 00:27:46.530
that tell the service mesh
what attributes to look for.

00:27:46.530 --> 00:27:48.750
So if you're doing
analytics, for example,

00:27:48.750 --> 00:27:50.420
what type of
analytics will I want

00:27:50.420 --> 00:27:52.700
to be sent to the API dashboard?

00:27:52.700 --> 00:27:55.070
I want things like
the request URI,

00:27:55.070 --> 00:27:57.036
the path, the type
of service that

00:27:57.036 --> 00:27:59.660
was accessed-- there are a whole
bunch of metrics that you want

00:27:59.660 --> 00:28:01.490
to collect, and the
definitions file

00:28:01.490 --> 00:28:03.830
defines that metric
for analytics.

00:28:03.830 --> 00:28:06.830
Similarly, if I'm defining
API keys or OAuth,

00:28:06.830 --> 00:28:08.660
where do I look
for these values,

00:28:08.660 --> 00:28:11.000
what values am I
interested in is what you

00:28:11.000 --> 00:28:13.100
define in the definitions file.

00:28:13.100 --> 00:28:17.150
This is a one-time setup up
that the mesh operator does.

00:28:17.150 --> 00:28:24.290
Once you're done with that,
the next is the handler.

00:28:24.290 --> 00:28:26.900
The handler creates
a configuration

00:28:26.900 --> 00:28:31.370
that allows Mixer to communicate
securely to the Apigee Edge

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:32.060
instance.

00:28:32.060 --> 00:28:34.190
Within Apigee Edge,
you'd have a tendency

00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:36.140
and so you've
defined that, but now

00:28:36.140 --> 00:28:38.780
you want some sort of
a secure way for Mixer

00:28:38.780 --> 00:28:42.860
to contact Apigee so
it can get those rules.

00:28:42.860 --> 00:28:48.530
And the last is when
do I apply this policy?

00:28:48.530 --> 00:28:51.210
I've taken a very
simplistic view here.

00:28:51.210 --> 00:28:54.770
This last step defines when
do I want this rule applied.

00:28:54.770 --> 00:28:56.630
In this case, I
said, well, any time

00:28:56.630 --> 00:28:59.596
there's an inbound
traffic, apply these rules.

00:28:59.596 --> 00:29:01.220
You may want to be
more selective here.

00:29:01.220 --> 00:29:03.680
You can say that,
well, apply this rule

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:06.590
only if the label is
API, or apply this rule

00:29:06.590 --> 00:29:09.440
only if the namespace is
API, or something like that.

00:29:09.440 --> 00:29:11.390
You can be more
creative with when you

00:29:11.390 --> 00:29:13.850
want this rule to be applied.

00:29:13.850 --> 00:29:16.685
All right, so now that I
have this rule applied--

00:29:16.685 --> 00:29:20.090
oh, that's not good.

00:29:20.090 --> 00:29:22.640
Let's try to access
that API again.

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:28.480
I got a permission
denied, quite as expected,

00:29:28.480 --> 00:29:30.180
because I applied
the rule that says

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:31.660
now I want to shut this down.

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:34.990
I do not want unauthorized
access to this API.

00:29:34.990 --> 00:29:40.030
So now when I try to access
it, it obviously shut me off.

00:29:40.030 --> 00:29:43.120
Normally what would happen now
is that this developer would

00:29:43.120 --> 00:29:45.355
go into a developer
portal and say, all right,

00:29:45.355 --> 00:29:47.380
I see the tracking
API right there.

00:29:47.380 --> 00:29:49.240
I'd like to request
access to this.

00:29:49.240 --> 00:29:51.340
And this developer
gets an API key

00:29:51.340 --> 00:29:53.620
and would use that
key now to access it.

00:29:53.620 --> 00:29:56.770
In the interest of time,
I've gone ahead and gotten

00:29:56.770 --> 00:29:59.770
one such API key and now
we're going to try and access

00:29:59.770 --> 00:30:03.340
this service with my API key.

00:30:03.340 --> 00:30:07.690
So same call, but with the
API key, except this time I

00:30:07.690 --> 00:30:10.010
am now able to
access that service.

00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:13.840
So the Mixer was able
to verify this API key.

00:30:13.840 --> 00:30:16.090
It was able to check
against the entitlements.

00:30:16.090 --> 00:30:18.040
So first, was the API key valid?

00:30:18.040 --> 00:30:22.270
Then, was this API key allowed
to access the tracking API,

00:30:22.270 --> 00:30:25.247
and then allow that request in?

00:30:25.247 --> 00:30:26.830
Also what happened
behind the scenes--

00:30:26.830 --> 00:30:28.560
and you'll see this
in a few minutes--

00:30:28.560 --> 00:30:32.830
Mixer sent telemetry
information about this API call

00:30:32.830 --> 00:30:35.800
to the API dashboard.

00:30:35.800 --> 00:30:38.830
Now more often
than not, API keys

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:41.800
are not sufficient
for enterprises.

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:44.260
They'd like to
secure it with OAuth.

00:30:44.260 --> 00:30:49.160
Let's do very quickly, if
we can also do OAuth here.

00:30:49.160 --> 00:30:52.480
So I've applied a spec that's
now going to implement or look

00:30:52.480 --> 00:30:53.950
for OAuth tokens.

00:30:53.950 --> 00:30:57.340
Let's take a look at what
that file looks like.

00:30:57.340 --> 00:31:01.870
This is the OAuth spec that I
have for the shipping service.

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:05.890
What you'll see here are
three pieces of info.

00:31:05.890 --> 00:31:08.650
One is which service
do I want protected.

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:11.200
That's the shipping
service, that's the target.

00:31:11.200 --> 00:31:16.030
And second, what issuer should
I look for in the JWT token,

00:31:16.030 --> 00:31:17.410
and you can see the URL there.

00:31:17.410 --> 00:31:19.370
That's the issuer
I'm interested in.

00:31:19.370 --> 00:31:22.690
And lastly, what's the JSON
web key endpoint I can get?

00:31:22.690 --> 00:31:24.840
That's where the
public keys are hosted.

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:26.960
That's hosted in
Apigee at this point.

00:31:26.960 --> 00:31:29.250
So how do I verify this token?

00:31:29.250 --> 00:31:33.220
Once this policy
is deployed, Envoy

00:31:33.220 --> 00:31:38.000
will now check if the
token is expired or not.

00:31:38.000 --> 00:31:40.900
And if it is not expired,
the public key is valid.

00:31:40.900 --> 00:31:44.230
I mean, does the digital
signature be valid.

00:31:44.230 --> 00:31:45.310
But that's one step.

00:31:45.310 --> 00:31:47.330
That's almost like the
authentication step.

00:31:47.330 --> 00:31:50.950
Make sure that your JSON
web token is reasonable.

00:31:50.950 --> 00:31:54.850
But that's every JSON web token
accessing every API, then.

00:31:54.850 --> 00:31:56.410
What about the next step?

00:31:56.410 --> 00:31:59.210
Can this user access this API?

00:31:59.210 --> 00:32:02.200
So now those attributes
are passed to Mixer.

00:32:02.200 --> 00:32:04.960
Mixer will still apply the
Apigee rules to say, all right,

00:32:04.960 --> 00:32:08.890
is this JSON web token allowed
to access the tracking API?

00:32:08.890 --> 00:32:11.270
And that's what we'll see next.

00:32:11.270 --> 00:32:14.970
So let's go ahead
and get a token.

00:32:14.970 --> 00:32:20.300
So I've got a script here that
will allow me to get a token.

00:32:20.300 --> 00:32:23.120
So let's do that and
store it in a variable.

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:27.180
Let's look at that JSON web
token, really long string,

00:32:27.180 --> 00:32:28.930
but I'm going to now
parse it and show you

00:32:28.930 --> 00:32:30.500
what that looks like.

00:32:30.500 --> 00:32:33.940
So let's go put that
in a file so you'll

00:32:33.940 --> 00:32:37.120
see what is in that token.

00:32:37.120 --> 00:32:42.790
And let's try that.

00:32:42.790 --> 00:32:45.980
OK, so you're seeing parts
of that JSON web token

00:32:45.980 --> 00:32:48.250
there because what
is inside that token

00:32:48.250 --> 00:32:52.510
is the API key or the client
identifier for that JSON web

00:32:52.510 --> 00:32:53.380
token.

00:32:53.380 --> 00:32:57.880
This then allows Mixer to check
if the JSON web token is really

00:32:57.880 --> 00:33:00.600
allowed to access
this particular API,

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:03.820
because you may have a rule
where this client ID can access

00:33:03.820 --> 00:33:06.490
a email service, but not
the tracking service.

00:33:06.490 --> 00:33:10.570
So that next step of entitlement
check is also done by Mixer.

00:33:10.570 --> 00:33:13.030
So let's now call
the same service

00:33:13.030 --> 00:33:18.385
using the access token
instead of the API key.

00:33:18.385 --> 00:33:21.610
I got back my JSON again.

00:33:21.610 --> 00:33:25.240
So what we've been able
to do is to protect

00:33:25.240 --> 00:33:29.080
the API while defining certain
rules in the service mesh.

00:33:29.080 --> 00:33:30.940
Well, what's also now
interesting to see

00:33:30.940 --> 00:33:34.077
is if I can get
this dashboard all

00:33:34.077 --> 00:33:37.240
to show up in the API
dashboard, because I

00:33:37.240 --> 00:33:38.350
want analytics for it.

00:33:38.350 --> 00:33:44.290
So I'm going to refresh this,
and I get hourly metrics, day,

00:33:44.290 --> 00:33:45.460
week, and so on.

00:33:45.460 --> 00:33:47.560
At the moment, I'm just
going to pick hour.

00:33:47.560 --> 00:33:50.230
I have a load generator
running, so you'll

00:33:50.230 --> 00:33:53.370
see many more requests than
I actually generated here.

00:33:53.370 --> 00:33:57.610
But I can dial down some of
the Istio-specific information

00:33:57.610 --> 00:34:00.760
and start looking at the
shipping API, the response

00:34:00.760 --> 00:34:03.740
times here and so on, but
also, more importantly,

00:34:03.740 --> 00:34:07.060
look at some of the
developer aspects of it.

00:34:07.060 --> 00:34:10.850
So who was the developer who
built this shipping service?

00:34:10.850 --> 00:34:13.480
How many errors does
this developer generate,

00:34:13.480 --> 00:34:15.100
so on and so forth,
is information

00:34:15.100 --> 00:34:19.889
I can get by looking
at the API dashboard.

00:34:19.889 --> 00:34:22.440
All right, back to the
presentation, please.

00:34:25.620 --> 00:34:30.210
OK, so to quickly summarize,
service management

00:34:30.210 --> 00:34:33.659
is needed for all your services,
and when you share those assets

00:34:33.659 --> 00:34:36.510
with other team, external
vendors, and partners,

00:34:36.510 --> 00:34:39.480
API management is
available for you.

00:34:39.480 --> 00:34:42.360
Now David for some
announcements.

00:34:42.360 --> 00:34:45.120
DAVID MUNRO: So this has
actually been mentioned before,

00:34:45.120 --> 00:34:48.489
but Istio 1.0 is literally
